From 405d46170a6d9077d4f0e9be4f978cc49cbb2bd3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arealjim <6187715+arealjim@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 22:59:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 56/56] runtime: composite equirect2 and cube layers into the
 eye before encode

XR_KHR_composition_layer_equirect2 and XR_KHR_composition_layer_cube were
not advertised, so apps drew those layers themselves or dropped them (360
video players, skyboxes, loading screens). Both are now advertised,
validated in EndFrame and composited into each eye's image before encode,
in submission order with the quad and cylinder layers, over or under the
projection layer as those are.

Quad and cylinder are rasterized surfaces; these two are ray cast: one
triangle covers the eye, each pixel's ray is taken back into the layer's
space through the inverse of its clip transform, and

- equirect2: a finite radius takes the far hit of the sphere from the eye
  (radius 0 or infinity: the direction itself); longitude 0 is -Z growing
  toward +X, s = longitude / central + 0.5; t = (upper - latitude) /
  (upper - lower), so the image's top row is the upper angle, as the spec
  says for asymmetric ranges too. Outside the angles nothing is drawn. The
  footprint filter of the quad applies, with the seam of a full sphere
  taking one tap;
- cube: the direction in the layer's orientation samples the cube texture
  (position ignored: the layer is infinitely far).

A cube layer on a non-cube swapchain, or the reverse, is skipped with one
warning. Equirect-only or cube-only frames (no projection layer) are not
streamed yet: the encoder still needs a projection layer.

Tests: the ray, the sphere hit and the image coordinates on the CPU; on
the GPU an infinite and a finite equirect, an angle window, and every
face of a cube turned by its orientation. GPU time per Frame-sized eye
(2048 px, [.overlay-bench]): a full-sphere 4096 x 2048 equirect 0.219 ms
p50, a 1024 cube 0.110 ms p50, over or under.
